{% set local_problem %}
<p>Priceville's varied terrain creates different drainage challenges throughout the community. Properties near the Tennessee River have naturally higher water tables. Newer developments may have grading issues from construction. Older properties often have drainage patterns that have shifted over decades.</p>

<p>Surface drainage solutions have limits. Splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. But on many Priceville properties, that's not far enough. Water that stays near your foundation — even a few feet away — can still find its way back during extended rainy periods.</p>

<p>We've seen the results throughout Morgan County: wet basements, crawlspace moisture, foundation cracks, and mold issues. Homeowners try various surface solutions without lasting success because the fundamental problem isn't being addressed.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this permanently. Instead of moving water a few feet and hoping for the best, buried pipes carry it far enough away that it can never threaten your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. The water exits at a predetermined location — typically at the property edge, into a drainage area, or into a dry well.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or solid PVC pipe, depending on your specific conditions. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away continuously. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater already in the soil), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff before it reaches the ground. They work with your gutter system to create complete water management.</p>
{% endset %}

